cartridge "DETONATION/B.BLACKMER/1986"

' DETONATION
' A ColecoVision game based on "Detonation" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' November 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, started by a
' two-line BASIC boot). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/detonation.dis, and watched running in an emulator),
' the game is built for the ColecoVision: a burning cord crawling round a
' bunker yard as a smooth spark sprite, steel bunkers and a white crate
' drawn in tiles, a bomb-disposal man as a two-colour sprite, spinning
' curls, sound effects and an ending.
'
' The game: a fuse burns round the edge of the yard towards the safe crate
' at the bottom. Three bombs sit in three steel bunkers. Fetch all three
' and bring them back into the crate before the fuse gets there; then the
' bombs are put back and the next round starts, with the guards faster.
' Each bunker's mouth is guarded by a spinning curl that sweeps across it.
' Touching a curl or a wall makes the fuse jump ahead. Ten rounds win.
'
' From the original: the fuse is one long cord for the whole game (it is
' not renewed between rounds); it moves one cell at a fixed time step and
' jumps one cell at once on every contact with the bunkers, the crate or a
' curl; three curls patrol the three bunker mouths together (two up and
' down in opposite directions, one left and right under the top bunker),
' over one shared sweep; touching a bomb takes it; with all three, standing
' in the crate finishes the round; every round the curls speed up (the C64
' moved them every 11th pass at round 1, then every 10th... every 2nd at
' round 10, SpdT here); ten rounds win; the winner's score counts the cells
' of fuse left; the fuse reaching the crate blows it up.
'
' Liberties: the yard is laid out on the 32 x 24 tile grid (the C64's
' 40 x 25 and expanded sprites); the cord has 76 cells (107 on the C64) and
' burns one every 2 seconds, the spark crawling smoothly between cells (the
' C64 jumped one cell every 4.3 s by the jiffy clock); a curl's touch costs
' a cell every 4 frames while it lasts (the C64 took one on every pass of
' its loop, a few hundred a second); walls are solid (the C64's man walked
' through them, burning fuse on every pass): walking straight into one
' costs a cell every 20 frames, sliding along it is free; the
' man walks 1.5 pixels a frame (on the C64 about 3, faster than its curls
' ever got); points during play (10 a bomb, 50 a round) as well as the
' final count of the fuse left (10 a cell, also given when the crate blows
' up, so a lost game still has a score); the bombs carried are shown at
' the top; the C64's background music (a tune that sped up each round) is
' replaced by a quiet tick of the burning cord; the C64's colour-cycling
' blast flashed many times a second: here the yard flashes at most twice
' a second; GAME OVER then the title (the C64 asked PRESS FIRE).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks (diagonals too). On the
' title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

